    "content": "Uganda has commenced the withdrawal of its military personnel from Somalia, according to an announcement by the country's army chief. The move signals the beginning of a security transition to Somali forces, who will assume full responsibility for safeguarding the nation. Uganda has been a key contributor to the African Union Mission in Somalia (AMISOM), now the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS), since 2007. Its troops have been involved in major operations against al-Shabaab and in securing population centers.\n\nThe decision to withdraw comes as part of a broader plan by the African Union and the Somali government to gradually reduce foreign military presence. The exact timeline and number of troops being pulled out have not been disclosed, but the army chief indicated that the process is underway. Uganda's contingent has been one of the largest within ATMIS, and its departure will require careful coordination to avoid security gaps.\n\nUganda's intervention in Somalia began in 2007 under AMISOM, with the aim of supporting the weak Somali government against insurgent groups. Over the years, Ugandan forces have suffered casualties, including high-profile attacks such as the 2017 assault on a base in Bulo Marer. The mission has evolved into a stabilization effort, with Uganda playing a leading role in training and mentoring Somali security forces.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the withdrawal raises questions about the country's security trajectory. Many in the diaspora have family members in Somalia and have invested in businesses and properties there. A smooth transition is crucial to maintaining stability, which could encourage further diaspora investment and return. Conversely, any deterioration in security could dampen these prospects and affect remittances, a vital lifeline for many families.\n\nThe withdrawal also comes amid ongoing discussions about the future of international support for Somalia. The Somali government has been working to build a capable national army, but challenges remain. The diaspora has often been called upon to contribute to peacebuilding and development efforts, and their role may become even more significant as external forces draw down.\n\nUganda's army chief did not provide a specific timeline for the completion of the withdrawal. The African Union and Somali authorities are expected to oversee the transition to ensure it does not compromise the gains made over the years. The international community has emphasized the need for a conditions-based approach to the drawdown, focusing on building sustainable Somali security capacity. Attribution: This article was written by the editorial team at Ramadpay Daily News and is based on reporting by Horseed Media.",
